From fd29525186e12ac43acf2e58e754472de245f206 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 05:16:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/49] A16: a repack worker reads one pack beside the pending
 saves of the pack before it

Port of T3's S020 work (teams/T3/S020/M004, repack completion overlap),
fitted to repair R2's read path. A repack worker waited for every
asynchronous save of a pack before it read the next pack. It now reads
one further pack while those saves finish, and never more than one:
the predecessor is settled before the pack after its successor is
read, and two packs are open together only if they hold at most 512
blobs between them.

What does not change: the number of workers; the shared 8 MiB budget
for plaintext copied for pending saves; the order of error precedence
(read error, save error, cancellation; in pack order); and the rule
that the worker returns, with or without an error, only after every
save it admitted has called back. A caller that saves the index and
deletes packs after a nil return therefore still has every blob
stored.

R2: the pack is read through loadBlobsFromPackReleased, as before this
commit. The handler never runs inside the backend's Load callback, so
a worker that waits for the save budget while two packs are open holds
no backend connection. T3's file called the coalesced stream; its
registration through a package-level function variable is not taken:
repack calls the loop directly.

New: internal/repository/repack_completion_overlap.go; T3's tests
(repack_completion_overlap_test.go, repack_completion_integration_test.go,
repack_budget_cancel_test.go, repack_successor_drain_test.go) and a new
model test, repack_completion_overlap_model_test.go: 20,000 random pack
sequences with read and save errors; at most one unsettled pack beside
the one being read, every pack's saves joined exactly once on every
path, the first failing pack's error returned, progress equal to the
packs settled without an error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
